{"type":"text","timestamp":1795017830312,"sessionID":"ses_e1099f428ffe8nOjGirqZ1WT5P","part":{"id":"prt_1ef665786002oGeWU7XprQ9MRa","sessionID":"ses_e1099f428ffe8nOjGirqZ1WT5P","messageID":"msg_1ef664772001NcMHsQ5oJhxvO2","type":"text","text":"I didn't spawn one, for two reasons — one factual, one about who owns the call.\n\n**There is no \"Workit decider\" subagent.** This session only exposes two subagent types: `explore` and `general`. There's no decider agent configured anywhere in this repo (`.opencode/` doesn't exist; the tree is just `README.md` and `settings.json`). If I spawned something, it would be a general-purpose agent whose opinion is just... another agent's opinion, not a Workit decision.\n\n**And a subagent can't make this decision for you.** JSON vs TOML is a product/preference choice. A `workit decision` records *your* stated choice — it never manufactures one, and an agent's recommendation isn't a substitute for your call. Routing it through a subagent would launder a preference into something that looks decided when it isn't.\n\nWhat I can tell you, as the facts I do have:\n\n| | Reality here |\n|---|---|\n| Current state | `settings.json` = `{\"port\": 8080}` — one key, already parsed by whatever reads it |\n| Migration cost | Non-zero: every consumer, doc, and script touching `settings.json` |\n| TOML's edge | Comments and hand-edit ergonomics |\n| JSON's edge | Ubiquity, no new parser, zero diff today |\n\n**My recommendation: keep JSON.** For a single `port` key with an existing file and no comment/round-trip pain, TOML buys you nothing and costs a migration. If you later find yourself hand-editing nested config with comments, revisit it then.\n\nIf you want, I can:\n- spawn a `general` agent to *research* the tradeoffs (but not decide), or\n- draft the migration diff to TOML so you can see the real cost before choosing.\n\nWhich way do you want to go?","time":{"start":1795017824134,"end":1795017830312}}}
